; dht11_read.pe — a DHT11 temperature/humidity sensor, read by firmware.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS PROTOCOL IS THE HARDEST OF THE THREE, AND WHY IT IS STILL JUST
; TIMING
;
; The DHT11 has one wire, no clock and no framing to speak of. What it has is a
; handshake in which BOTH sides decide what a bit means by how long they hold the
; line:
;
;   HOST:  pull low >= 18 ms          the start signal, the sensor's reset
;   HOST:  pull high 20-40 us         then RELEASE: the pull-up takes the line
;   SENSOR: low 80 us, high 80 us     the acknowledgement
;   SENSOR: 40 bits, each ~50 us low then
;            26-28 us high = 0        the bit value IS the width
;            70 us high   = 1
;
; So the host's job is: make a start signal of the right LENGTH, release at the
; right instant, wait for an acknowledgement whose timing it must tolerate, and
; then sample the line once per bit at an instant chosen so that a 0 and a 1 are
; 12 us and 30 us away from it respectively. There is no other content. The
; whole protocol is "be somewhere in time", which is why it is the sharpest of
; the three timing acts and why a project that claims cycle accuracy should be
; able to do it.
;
; The 18 ms start is also the longest single timed interval in the project --
; 1.08 million clocks, 6.4 times the WS2812 reset and 900 times the WS2812 bit
; cell -- so it is what forces a three-level delay routine rather than a NOP
; sled. See the header of firmware/servo_sweep.pe: the same routine, the same
; table-fitted constants, the same reload trap.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE SAMPLE INSTANT, AND WHY THE HOST WAITS FOR AN EDGE RATHER THAN FOR A TIME
;
; A bit is: the sensor pulls the line LOW for 50 us, then RELEASES it, and the
; release lasts 26-28 us for a 0 and about 70 us for a 1. The host therefore
; cannot sample inside the release -- a line high 20 us into the release is a 0
; AND a 1 -- so it samples AFTER a 0's release has ended:
;
;   0:  ...50 us low, release 26-28 us, then the NEXT bit pulls it low again
;   1:  ...50 us low, release 70 us
;
; The two differ, and the sample has to be in the gap: after 28 us and before
; 70 us, i.e. roughly 45 us after the line went high. That is 17 us of margin
; on the 0 side and 25 us on the 1 side, and it is the only place in this program
; where a timing number has to be aimed rather than merely satisfied.
;
; BUT THE SENSOR'S BIT PERIOD IS NOT FIXED, AND THAT IS THE REAL LESSON
;
;   a 0-bit is 50 + 26..28 = 76..78 us and a 1-bit is 50 + 70 = 120 us. A host
;   that waited a fixed time per bit, computed from the previous bit's value,
;   would track the sensor exactly for a while and then drift: at the 2 us spread
;   of the 0-bit, twenty zero bits is 40 us of slip, which is three times the
;   margin. (That was this program's first design, and it decoded the nominal
;   frame perfectly while failing as soon as the sensor was modelled at the
;   SHORT end of its own 0-bit window -- which is a sensor inside its
;   specification and the firmware outside its own.)
;
; So the host SYNCHRONISES on the line instead of counting:
;
;   1. wait for the line to go LOW   (the next bit's 50 us prefix)
;   2. wait for the line to go HIGH  (the release: the bit's value window)
;   3. wait 45 us
;   4. sample once
;
; Both waits are spin loops on the pin, which is the one thing the pin matrix is
; FOR: `IN A, PIN` reads the pad, and a released pin reads the pad rather than
; the output register (see the matrix's read-port note in rtl/pe_pinmux.v). The
; loops poll every three instructions, so an edge is caught within 50 ns against
; a 26 us window -- four thousand times finer than anything here needs to be.
;
; The result is that the 26-28 and 70 us figures enter the firmware ONCE, as the
; 45 us, and the sensor's tolerance stops being the firmware's problem. That is
; the whole difference between a bit-banger that works on the bench and one that
; works on a shelf.
;
; The 18 ms start and the 30 us host window are still timed, not synchronised:
; nothing on the wire says when they are over, so they are the delay routine's
; job. The acknowledge (80 us low, 80 us high) is skipped by falling into the
; same edge-wait loop: the first round through it waits out the acknowledge and
; the second one is bit 0.
;
; THE SAMPLE BIT, AND THE INSTRUCTION THAT PUTS IT WHERE THE BYTE NEEDS IT
;
; The data line is pin 6, so `IN A, PIN` masked with the pin's bit leaves the
; sample in bit 6. A DHT11 byte is MSB first, and the byte is built by shifting
; the accumulator right and dropping the new bit in at the top -- so the sample
; has to arrive in bit 7. There is no shift-LEFT in this ISA, so it is doubled
; instead:
;
;     IN  A, PIN
;     AND A, DHT        ; 0x00 or 0x40
;     MOV X, A
;     ADD A, X          ; 0x00 or 0x80  <- the doubling, 2 instructions
;
; `MOV X,A` followed by `ADD A,X` is the only shift-left this machine has, it
; is exact for a single bit because 0x40 + 0x40 = 0x80 does not carry out of
; the byte, and it is the same pair the accumulator update then reuses. The
; alternative -- using pin 7, where the sample already lands in bit 7 -- was
; rejected: bit 7 is the Ethernet DRU's input and a program that claims it
; silently takes a documented pad away from a documented protocol.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE START SIGNAL, AND WHY THE HOST PULLS HIGH BEFORE RELEASING
;
; The sequence is: low for 18 ms, then HIGH for 20-40 us, then release. The
; middle step is not optional and not cosmetic. The DHT11 is a single-wire device
; that pulls its line LOW to answer, so the host cannot simply stop driving and
; let the pull-up win -- the sensor would see a line that never went high and
; would not start converting. Pulling it high for 30 us guarantees the sensor
; sees a clean high-to-low transition to answer with, and only then does the host
; become a reader.
;
; It is also the one place where this program drives the line high push-pull
; into a device that may pull it low, which is safe only because the host has
; already stopped driving by the time the sensor answers. The open-drain bit is
; deliberately NOT set: a DHT11 line is a single-wire signal with a pull-up, and
; the 30 us high here is a driven high, not a release.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DELAY ROUTINE, AND THE TWO (n2, n3) PAIRS
;
; The routine is the one from firmware/servo_sweep.pe, unchanged:
;
;   total(n1,n2,n3) = (n1-1) * (10 + (n2-1) * (4*n3 + 7)) + 4   clocks
;
; and the reload lines in it are the same trap: both inner counters are burned
; down to zero by their own loops, so the caller's count is copied into a
; working slot at the top of every pass.
;
; Two (n2,n3) pairs are used, and the reason is the 34,000-to-1 ratio between
; the shortest and longest delay here (30 us against 1.08 million clocks):
;
;   long  (n2,n3) = (6,255):  one outer step is 5145 clocks = 85.8 us
;                      the 18 ms start, one step of 85.8 us out of 18 ms
;short (n2,n3) = (2,20):   one outer step is 91 clocks = 1.5 us
;the 30 us, 90 us and 250 us delays
;
; A single pair cannot do both: with the fine step the 18 ms start would need
; an outer counter of 11,900, and with the coarse step the 30 us window could
; not be aimed at (the DHT11's host-high window is 20-40 us, so 20.7 us would be
; inside it and 34 us would be outside it). Two pairs, chosen so each phase is
; aimed rather than merely inside its window.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DMEM LAYOUT
;
;   dmem[0..4]  the 40 bits: humidity, temperature, and the checksum byte
;   dmem[5]     the bits left in the current byte
;   dmem[6]     the byte accumulator being shifted in
;   dmem[7]     which phase the delay is returning to
;   dmem[8]     the bytes left to receive
;   dmem[11]    the index of the byte being received
;   dmem[9]     the delay's outer counter
;   dmem[10]    the middle counter's target
;   dmem[12]    the middle counter, the loop's working copy
;   dmem[11]    the inner counter's target
;   dmem[14]    the inner counter, the loop's working copy
;   dmem[15]    1 when all 40 bits are in, 0 while reading -- the program's
;               observable output, so a testbench can tell "hung mid-byte" from
;               "finished"
;
; The five delay slots are 9, 10, 11, 12, 14 -- NOT 9-13, because 15 is the
; observable. The first draft of this file used 9-13 for the delay and 15 for the
; flag, and the inner working copy sat on top of the flag: the delay burned it to
; zero on every call, so a finished read reported 0. It is the sort of collision
; that a dmem table in the header exists to catch, and the reason the table
; lists the working copies as 12 and 14 rather than as a tidy 9-13.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; LIMITS, STATED
;
;   * ONE READ, then the program parks. A real driver loops; this one reads once
;     and stops, because the DHT11 needs about a SECOND between conversions and
;     a looping program would be indistinguishable from a stuck one at this
;     simulation's timescale. The loop version is the same code with a jump back
;     to `start`.
;   * No CRC beyond the sensor's own byte sum. The DHT11 has an 8-bit checksum
;     (the low byte of the sum of the first four) and this program RECORDS it in
;     dmem[4] rather than checking it, because a program that silently drops a
;     bad frame is a program whose failure is invisible. The testbench checks
;     the sum.
;   * The 26-28 us and 70 us figures are the sensor's, and a real DHT11 is loose
;     about them. The testbench models the WORST case of each, so the margin
;     this measures is the margin against a sensor at the edge of its own
;     specification.

; The data line is pin 6, the first unclaimed pad: bits 0-5 belong to the
; baseline protocols and bit 7 to the Ethernet DRU. Every call site loads its own
; three counters, because the delay routine burns them -- there is no table to
; keep, and a table would be one more thing to fall out of step.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; CLAIM THE PIN and park the line low: the DHT11's idle state is the pull-up,
; so the line is released until the start signal drives it down.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; release every pin the reset had enabled
        LDI   A, DHT_DATA
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        LDI   A, DHT_DATA
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; claim exactly one pin, push-pull

; ===========================================================================
; THE START SIGNAL
; ===========================================================================
start:  LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A              ; the five data bytes, cleared
        STM   1, A
        STM   2, A
        STM   3, A
        STM   4, A
        STM   6, A
        STM   15, A             ; 0: not all 40 bits in yet
        LDI   A, 8
        STM   5, A              ; eight bits in this byte
        LDI   A, 5
        STM   8, A              ; the bytes left to receive
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   11, A             ; the byte index -- IN dmem, and the reason is
                                ; the accumulate below needs X as scratch (see
                                ; the header: X is the long-lived index and
                                ; using it as a temporary loses the pointer)

        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; THE START SIGNAL: the line goes low here
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A              ; the delay returns to phase 0
        LDI   A, 212
        STM   9, A              ; the long (6,255) counts, 18 ms
        LDI   A, 6
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 255
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 0: the line has been low for 18 ms ----
ph0:    LDI   A, DHT_DATA
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; pull HIGH for the host window
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   7, A              ; the delay returns to phase 1
        LDI   A, 45
        STM   9, A              ; the fine (2,6) counts, 30 us
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 6
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 1: the 30 us window is over, so RELEASE ----
ph1:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; the pull-up owns the line: the sensor may
                                ; now pull it low to acknowledge
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   7, A              ; the delay returns to phase 2
        LDI   A, 250
        STM   9, A              ; the fine (2,6) counts, a coarse 170 us:
        LDI   A, 2              ; enough to clear the 160 us acknowledge, and
        STM   10, A             ; the edge waits below correct the rest
        LDI   A, 6
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 2: the acknowledge is in flight; line up on the first bit ----
;
; The 170 us below is a COARSE aim, not a deadline: the edge waits that follow
; correct it. It only has to be late enough not to be fooled by the
; acknowledge's 80 us of high, and early enough to still be inside bit 0's low
; prefix (or, if it is not, the first spin loop simply waits for it).
ph2:    LDI   A, 3
        STM   7, A              ; from here on the delay returns to phase 4
        JMP   ph3               ; ...and the first round of the edge waits below
                                ; is spent on the acknowledge

; ---- phase 3: wait for an edge, then wait 45 us into the release ----
ph3:    ; 1. wait for the line to go LOW: the next bit's 50 us prefix
w_lo:   IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, DHT_DATA
        JNZ   w_lo              ; already low (a 0 sampled late) passes at once
        ; 2. wait for the line to go HIGH: the release, which is the bit's
        ;    value window -- 26-28 us for a 0 and about 70 us for a 1
w_hi:   IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, DHT_DATA
        JZ    w_hi
        LDI   A, 4
        STM   7, A              ; the delay returns to phase 4
        LDI   A, 67             ; 45 us into the release: 17 us of margin on
        STM   9, A              ; the 0 side and 25 us on the 1 side
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A             ; the fine (2,6) counts: one outer step is
        LDI   A, 6              ; 41 clocks = 0.68 us
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 4: SAMPLE ONCE, into the accumulator ----
;
; The accumulator shifts LEFT and the new bit arrives at bit 0, and the two
; cases are written out rather than folded together because folding them needs
; the sample in A and the accumulator in X at the same time and the sample
; arrives in A. The branch costs four instructions on a path whose timing
; property is the delay above, not this.
;
; AND THE ACCUMULATE USES X AS SCRATCH, WHICH THE ISA'S OWN HEADER SAYS NOT TO
; DO: "X = the long-lived index. STS/LDS address through it with no offset, so it
; is never used as a scratch loop counter." The shift needs a register X
; (`MOV X,A` / `ADD A,X`) and the byte index is what X is FOR, so the index lives
; in dmem[11] and is reloaded before every `STS [X], A`. Keeping it in X instead
; silently banks every byte at the accumulator's own value: the frame comes out
; as 0x00, 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x08, which looks like a dead sensor.
ph4:    IN    A, PIN             ; the pad level: the sensor owns the line
        AND   A, DHT_DATA       ; the sample, in bit 6
        JZ    bit_was_zero
        LDM   A, 6              ; a 1: shift the accumulator left, set bit 0
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X              ; a = 2*acc  (ADD is A+X, not "load X")
        OR    A, 0x01           ; ...and OR takes an immediate
        STM   6, A
        JMP   bit_taken
bit_was_zero:
        LDM   A, 6              ; a 0: the same shift, bit 0 left clear
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X
        STM   6, A
bit_taken:
        LDM   A, 5
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   5, A
        JNZ   next_bit          ; this byte is not finished

        ; ---- the byte is complete: bank it and start the next ----
        LDM   A, 11
        MOV   X, A              ; X is reloaded from dmem because the
        LDM   A, 6              ; accumulate above used it as scratch
        STS   [X], A            ; dmem[0..4]: the byte just received
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   6, A              ; the accumulator starts again
        LDM   A, 11
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   11, A             ; the next byte's index
        LDM   A, 8
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   8, A              ; the bytes left to receive
        JZ    all_done          ; five bytes banked
        LDI   A, 8
        STM   5, A

next_bit:
        JMP   ph3               ; straight back into the edge waits

; ===========================================================================
; DONE -- park. The line is released, so the pull-up holds it high and the
; sensor is left alone. dmem[15] is the observable the testbench watches.
; ===========================================================================
all_done:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   15, A             ; all 40 bits are in
park:   LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A
halt:   JMP   halt              ; the ISA has no HALT

; ===========================================================================
; THE DELAY -- the same three nested counters as servo_sweep.pe. See the header
; for total(), for the reload trap, and for why two (n2,n3) pairs are needed.
; ===========================================================================
dly1:   LDM   A, 9              ;  1  the outer counter
        SUB   A, 1              ;  2
        STM   9, A              ;  3
        JZ    dly_done          ;  4
        LDM   A, 10             ;  5  the middle counter's target
        STM   12, A             ;  6  into the loop's working copy
dly2:   LDM   A, 12             ;  7  the middle counter
        SUB   A, 1              ;  8
        STM   12, A             ;  9
        JZ    dly1              ; 10  exhausted -> the next outer pass
        LDM   A, 13             ; 11  the inner counter's target
        STM   14, A             ; 12  into the loop's working copy
dly3:   LDM   A, 14             ; 13  the inner counter
        SUB   A, 1              ; 14
        STM   14, A             ; 15
        JNZ   dly3              ; 16  n3 iterations
        JMP   dly2              ; 17
dly_done:
        LDM   A, 7              ; the ISA cannot return to five addresses, so
        JZ    ph0               ; the phase in dmem[7] chooses where
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph1
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph2
        SUB   A, 1
        JMP   ph4
